<!DOCTYPE html>
<html dir="ltr" lang="<%= I18n.locale %>">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1">
  <title><%= page_title %></title>

  <%# Every page had a title and nothing else, so a link shared anywhere -- a
      search result, Telegram, a forum post -- showed the URL and no summary.
      @meta_description lets a controller say something specific; the fallback
      describes the project. %>
  <meta name="description" content="<%= @meta_description || t('site.default_meta_description') %>">
  <meta name="theme-color" content="#0f1422">

  <%# Path only, no query string. The canonical is now the page's OWN address
      in its own language: since #154 put the locale in the path, /ru/donate is
      a different page from /donate rather than a rendering of it, and pointing
      them all at the English one would ask search engines to drop two thirds
      of the site -- which is the state this change exists to end. hreflang
      below is what tells them the three are translations of each other. %>
  <link rel="canonical" href="<%= request.base_url + request.path %>">

  <%# Every alternate names every other AND itself; a set where one page omits
      itself is not a set, and search engines discard the lot. x-default is the
      one to serve a visitor whose language is none of these. %>
  <% locale_alternates.each do |code, path| %>
    <link rel="alternate" hreflang="<%= code %>" href="<%= request.base_url + path %>">
  <% end %>
  <link rel="alternate" hreflang="x-default" href="<%= request.base_url + locale_alternates[I18n.default_locale] %>">

  <meta property="og:site_name" content="OpenIPC">
  <meta property="og:type" content="website">
  <meta property="og:title" content="<%= page_title %>">
  <meta property="og:description" content="<%= @meta_description || t('site.default_meta_description') %>">
  <meta property="og:url" content="<%= request.base_url + request.path %>">
  <%# Absolute, because crawlers do not resolve relative og:image. In public/
      rather than the pipeline: OG images are fetched once and cached by URL by
      every consumer, so a fingerprint would only invalidate those caches. %>
  <meta property="og:image" content="<%= request.base_url %>/og-default.png">
  <meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image">

  <%= csrf_meta_tags %>
  <%= csp_meta_tag %>
  <link rel="icon" type="image/x-icon" href="<%= asset_path 'favicon.png' %>">
  <%# The two faces every page renders body text in. Without the hint they are
      discovered only when the stylesheet has parsed, which is a round trip of
      invisible text. crossorigin is required even same-origin: fonts are
      fetched in CORS mode, and a preload without it is fetched twice. %>
  <link rel="preload" href="/fonts/ibm-plex-sans-latin-400-normal.woff2" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin>
  <link rel="preload" href="/fonts/ibm-plex-sans-latin-600-normal.woff2" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin>
  <%= stylesheet_link_tag 'application' %>
  <%= javascript_include_tag 'application', defer: true %>
  <%# First-party page counting (#181). Same-origin by construction: nginx
      proxies /api/a/c.js and /api/a/count to GoatCounter on 127.0.0.1:8081,
      and nothing leaves the host. Kept under /api/ because #142 has the
      mirrors proxy only /api/, /wall/i/ and /dl/firmware/ to the origin, so
      this needs no edge change when they become edge nodes in #167.

      /api/a/c.js rather than count.js on purpose: filter lists match the
      count.js pattern even when it is self-hosted, and a first-party path
      with no "count", "track" or "analytics" in it is the documented remedy.

      AFTER the bundle, and `defer` like it. Deferred scripts run in document
      order, so src/analytics.js has set window.goatcounter -- including
      no_onload -- before this file looks for it. `async` here would be a race
      this loses silently, by counting the load event once per session instead
      of once per page. %>
  <script src="/api/a/c.js" data-goatcounter="/api/a/count" defer></script>
</head>

<body id="page-top" class="<%= css_debug %>">
<a class="visually-hidden-focusable" href="#main">Skip to content</a>
<%= render 'layouts/navbar' %>

<main id="main">
  <%# Pages opt out of the .container wrapper with `content_for :fullwidth` when
      they lay out their own full-bleed sections. Flash messages still need the
      wrapper, or they would run edge to edge. Existing pages set nothing and
      keep the wrapper they have always had. %>
  <% if content_for?(:fullwidth) %>
    <% flashes = display_flashes %>
    <% if flashes.present? %>
      <div class="container"><%= flashes %></div>
    <% end %>
    <%= yield %>
  <% else %>
    <div class="container mb-4">
      <%= display_flashes %>
      <%= yield %>
    </div>
  <% end %>
</main>

<%= render 'layouts/footer' %>
</body>
</html>
